    "answer": {
      "short": "Multiplying by $\\sin\\theta$ and applying product-to-sum gives a telescoping sum equal to $\\tfrac12(1-\\cos2n\\theta)=\\sin^2(n\\theta)$.\n\nDivide by $\\sin\\theta$ to obtain the identity.",
      "worked": "Let $S=\\sin\\theta+\\sin3\\theta+\\cdots+\\sin((2n-1)\\theta)$. Using $\\sin A\\sin B=\\tfrac12[\\cos(A-B)-\\cos(A+B)]$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sin\\theta\\,S&=\\sum_{k=1}^{n}\\sin((2k-1)\\theta)\\sin\\theta\\\\\\sin\\theta\\,S&=\\frac12\\sum_{k=1}^{n}\\left[\\cos((2k-2)\\theta)-\\cos(2k\\theta)\\right]\\\\\\sin\\theta\\,S&=\\frac12\\left[\\cos0-\\cos2\\theta+\\cos2\\theta-\\cos4\\theta+\\cdots+\\cos((2n-2)\\theta)-\\cos(2n\\theta)\\right]\\\\\\sin\\theta\\,S&=\\frac12[1-\\cos(2n\\theta)]\\\\\\sin\\theta\\,S&=\\sin^2(n\\theta).\\end{align*}$$\nThe intermediate cosine terms cancel. Since $\\sin\\theta\\ne0$, division gives\n$$\\sin\\theta+\\sin3\\theta+\\cdots+\\sin((2n-1)\\theta)=\\frac{\\sin^2(n\\theta)}{\\sin\\theta}.$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
